    {
      "id": "b1.5.12",
      "n": 12,
      "prompt": "$17 \\equiv 2$ and $8 \\equiv 3$ (mod 5). Check that $17 + 8 \\equiv 2 + 3$ and $17 \\cdot 8 \\equiv 2 \\cdot 3$ (mod 5).",
      "answer": {
        "type": "self-check"
      },
      "answerText": "25 − 5 = 20 and 136 − 6 = 130, both divisible by 5",
      "solution": [
        "17 + 8 = 25 and 2 + 3 = 5. 25 − 5 = 20, divisible by 5.",
        "17 · 8 = 136 and 2 · 3 = 6. 136 − 6 = 130, divisible by 5."
      ],
      "langType": "§4 ex. 24–25",
      "stretch": true
    },
    {
      "id": "b1.5.13",
      "n": 13,
      "prompt": "Prove: if $n^2$ is divisible by 3, then $n$ is divisible by 3. Use $3k$, $3k + 1$, $3k + 2$.",
      "answer": {
        "type": "self-check"
      },
      "answerText": "Square each form",
      "solution": [
        "If n = 3k + 1, then n² = 9k² + 6k + 1 = 3(3k² + 2k) + 1. This is not divisible by 3.",
        "If n = 3k + 2, then n² = 9k² + 12k + 4 = 3(3k² + 4k + 1) + 1. This is not divisible by 3.",
        "So n must have the form 3k."
      ],
